@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.850 = private unnamed_addr constant [25 x i8] c"unreliable-callable-check", align 1
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.851 = private unnamed_addr constant [911 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `hasattr` to test if an object is callable (e.g.,\0A`hasattr(obj, \22__call__\22)`).\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AUsing `hasattr` is an unreliable mechanism for testing if an object is\0Acallable. If `obj` implements a custom `__getattr__`, or if its `__call__`\0Ais itself not callable, you may get misleading results.\0A\0AInstead, use `callable(obj)` to test if `obj` is callable.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Ahasattr(obj, \22__call__\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Acallable(obj)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `callable`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#callable)\0A- [Python documentation: `hasattr`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#hasattr)\0A- [Python documentation: `__getattr__`](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/datamodel.html#object.__getattr__)\0A- [Python documentation: `__call__`](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/datamodel.html#object.__call__)\0A", align 1
