@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.864 = private unnamed_addr constant [883 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `zip` calls without an explicit `strict` parameter.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ABy default, if the iterables passed to `zip` are of different lengths, the\0Aresulting iterator will be silently truncated to the length of the shortest\0Aiterable. This can lead to subtle bugs.\0A\0APass `strict=True` to raise a `ValueError` if the iterables are of\0Anon-uniform length. Alternatively, if the iterables are deliberately of\0Adifferent lengths, pass `strict=False` to make the intention explicit.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Azip(a, b)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Azip(a, b, strict=True)\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe for `zip` calls that contain\0A`**kwargs`, as adding a `strict` keyword argument to such a call may lead\0Ato a duplicate keyword argument error.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `zip`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#zip)\0A", align 1
