@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.881 = private unnamed_addr constant [954 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for usage of `datetime.datetime.utcnow()`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0APython datetime objects can be naive or timezone-aware. While an aware\0Aobject represents a specific moment in time, a naive object does not\0Acontain enough information to unambiguously locate itself relative to other\0Adatetime objects. Since this can lead to errors, it is recommended to\0Aalways use timezone-aware objects.\0A\0A`datetime.datetime.utcnow()` returns a naive datetime object; instead, use\0A`datetime.datetime.now(tz=...)` to create a timezone-aware object.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport datetime\0A\0Adatetime.datetime.utcnow()\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport datetime\0A\0Adatetime.datetime.now(tz=datetime.timezone.utc)\0A```\0A\0AOr, for Python 3.11 and later:\0A```python\0Aimport datetime\0A\0Adatetime.datetime.now(tz=datetime.UTC)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: Aware and Naive Objects](https://docs.python.org/3/library/datetime.html#aware-and-naive-objects)\0A", align 1
