@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.888 = private unnamed_addr constant [31 x i8] c"future-required-type-annotation", align 1
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.889 = private unnamed_addr constant [1315 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of PEP 585- and PEP 604-style type annotations in Python\0Amodules that lack the required `from __future__ import annotations` import\0Afor compatibility with older Python versions.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AUsing PEP 585 and PEP 604 style annotations without a `from __future__ import\0Aannotations` import will cause runtime errors on Python versions prior to\0A3.9 and 3.10, respectively.\0A\0ABy adding the `__future__` import, the interpreter will no longer interpret\0Aannotations at evaluation time, making the code compatible with both past\0Aand future Python versions.\0A\0AThis rule respects the [`target-version`] setting. For example, if your\0Aproject targets Python 3.10 and above, adding `from __future__ import annotations`\0Adoes not impact your ability to leverage PEP 604-style unions (e.g., to\0Aconvert `Optional[str]` to `str | None`). As such, this rule will only\0Aflag such usages if your project targets Python 3.9 or below.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Adef func(obj: dict[str, int | None]) -> None: ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Afrom __future__ import annotations\0A\0A\0Adef func(obj: dict[str, int | None]) -> None: ...\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as adding `from __future__ import annotations`\0Amay change the semantics of the program.\0A\0A## Options\0A- `target-version`\0A", align 1
