@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.910 = private unnamed_addr constant [1190 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `startswith` or `endswith` calls on the same value with\0Adifferent prefixes or suffixes.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe `startswith` and `endswith` methods accept tuples of prefixes or\0Asuffixes respectively. Passing a tuple of prefixes or suffixes is more\0Aefficient and readable than calling the method multiple times.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Amsg = \22Hello, world!\22\0Aif msg.startswith(\22Hello\22) or msg.startswith(\22Hi\22):\0A    print(\22Greetings!\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Amsg = \22Hello, world!\22\0Aif msg.startswith((\22Hello\22, \22Hi\22)):\0A    print(\22Greetings!\22)\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is unsafe, as in some cases, it will be unable to determine\0Awhether the argument to an existing `.startswith` or `.endswith` call is a\0Atuple. For example, given `msg.startswith(x) or msg.startswith(y)`, if `x`\0Aor `y` is a tuple, and the semantic model is unable to detect it as such,\0Athe rule will suggest `msg.startswith((x, y))`, which will error at\0Aruntime.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `str.startswith`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#str.startswith)\0A- [Python documentation: `str.endswith`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#str.endswith)\0A", align 1
