@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.937 = private unnamed_addr constant [1656 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `__iter__` methods in stubs that return `Iterable[T]` instead\0Aof an `Iterator[T]`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`__iter__` methods should always should return an `Iterator` of some kind,\0Anot an `Iterable`.\0A\0AIn Python, an `Iterable` is an object that has an `__iter__` method; an\0A`Iterator` is an object that has `__iter__` and `__next__` methods. All\0A`__iter__` methods are expected to return `Iterator`s. Type checkers may\0Anot always recognize an object as being iterable if its `__iter__` method\0Adoes not return an `Iterator`.\0A\0AEvery `Iterator` is an `Iterable`, but not every `Iterable` is an `Iterator`.\0AFor example, `list` is an `Iterable`, but not an `Iterator`; you can obtain\0Aan iterator over a list's elements by passing the list to `iter()`:\0A\0A```pycon\0A>>> import collections.abc\0A>>> x = [42]\0A>>> isinstance(x, collections.abc.Iterable)\0ATrue\0A>>> isinstance(x, collections.abc.Iterator)\0AFalse\0A>>> next(x)\0ATraceback (most recent call last):\0A File \22<stdin>\22, line 1, in <module>\0ATypeError: 'list' object is not an iterator\0A>>> y = iter(x)\0A>>> isinstance(y, collections.abc.Iterable)\0ATrue\0A>>> isinstance(y, collections.abc.Iterator)\0ATrue\0A>>> next(y)\0A42\0A```\0A\0AUsing `Iterable` rather than `Iterator` as a return type for an `__iter__`\0Amethods would imply that you would not necessarily be able to call `next()`\0Aon the returned object, violating the expectations of the interface.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Aimport collections.abc\0A\0A\0Aclass Klass:\0A    def __iter__(self) -> collections.abc.Iterable[str]: ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Aimport collections.abc\0A\0A\0Aclass Klass:\0A    def __iter__(self) -> collections.abc.Iterator[str]: ...\0A```\0A", align 1
