@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.967 = private unnamed_addr constant [792 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for static `str.split` calls that can be replaced with list literals.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AList literals are more readable and do not require the overhead of calling `str.split`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0A\22a,b,c,d\22.split(\22,\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0A[\22a\22, \22b\22, \22c\22, \22d\22]\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe for implicit string concatenations with comments interleaved\0Abetween segments, as comments may be removed.\0A\0AFor example, the fix would be marked as unsafe in the following case:\0A```python\0A(\0A    \22a\22  # comment\0A    \22,\22  # comment\0A    \22b\22  # comment\0A).split(\22,\22)\0A```\0A\0Aas this is converted to `[\22a\22, \22b\22]` without any of the comments.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `str.split`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#str.split)\0A", align 1
