@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.986 = private unnamed_addr constant [952 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for the presence of string literals in `X | Y`-style union types.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A[PEP 604] introduced a new syntax for union type annotations based on the\0A`|` operator.\0A\0AWhile Python's type annotations can typically be wrapped in strings to\0Aavoid runtime evaluation, the use of a string member within an `X | Y`-style\0Aunion type will cause a runtime error.\0A\0AInstead, remove the quotes, wrap the _entire_ union in quotes, or use\0A`from __future__ import annotations` to disable runtime evaluation of\0Aannotations entirely.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Avar: str | \22int\22\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Avar: str | int\0A```\0A\0AOr, extend the quotes to include the entire union:\0A```python\0Avar: \22str | int\22\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [PEP 563 - Postponed Evaluation of Annotations](https://peps.python.org/pep-0563/)\0A- [PEP 604 \E2\80\93 Allow writing union types as `X | Y`](https://peps.python.org/pep-0604/)\0A\0A[PEP 604]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0604/\0A", align 1
