@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.990 = private unnamed_addr constant [1234 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for the use of legacy `np.random` function calls.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AAccording to the NumPy documentation's [Legacy Random Generation]:\0A\0A> The `RandomState` provides access to legacy generators... This class\0A> should only be used if it is essential to have randoms that are\0A> identical to what would have been produced by previous versions of\0A> NumPy.\0A\0AThe members exposed directly on the `random` module are convenience\0Afunctions that alias to methods on a global singleton `RandomState`\0Ainstance. NumPy recommends using a dedicated `Generator` instance\0Arather than the random variate generation methods exposed directly on\0Athe `random` module, as the new `Generator` is both faster and has\0Abetter statistical properties.\0A\0ASee the documentation on [Random Sampling] and [NEP 19] for further\0Adetails.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport numpy as np\0A\0Anp.random.seed(1337)\0Anp.random.normal()\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Arng = np.random.default_rng(1337)\0Arng.normal()\0A```\0A\0A[Legacy Random Generation]: https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/random/legacy.html#legacy\0A[Random Sampling]: https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/random/index.html#random-quick-start\0A[NEP 19]: https://numpy.org/neps/nep-0019-rng-policy.html\0A", align 1
