@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1133 = private unnamed_addr constant [1486 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for the use of generics that can be replaced with standard library\0Avariants based on [PEP 585].\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A[PEP 585] enabled collections in the Python standard library (like `list`)\0Ato be used as generics directly, instead of importing analogous members\0Afrom the `typing` module (like `typing.List`).\0A\0AWhen available, the [PEP 585] syntax should be used instead of importing\0Amembers from the `typing` module, as it's more concise and readable.\0AImporting those members from `typing` is considered deprecated as of [PEP\0A585].\0A\0AThis rule is enabled when targeting Python 3.9 or later (see:\0A[`target-version`]). By default, it's _also_ enabled for earlier Python\0Aversions if `from __future__ import annotations` is present, as\0A`__future__` annotations are not evaluated at runtime. If your code relies\0Aon runtime type annotations (either directly or via a library like\0APydantic), you can disable this behavior for Python versions prior to 3.9\0Aby setting [`lint.pyupgrade.keep-runtime-typing`] to `true`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom typing import List\0A\0Afoo: List[int] = [1, 2, 3]\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afoo: list[int] = [1, 2, 3]\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as it may lead to runtime errors when\0Aalongside libraries that rely on runtime type annotations, like Pydantic,\0Aon Python versions prior to Python 3.9.\0A\0A## Options\0A- `target-version`\0A- `lint.pyupgrade.keep-runtime-typing`\0A\0A[PEP 585]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0585/\0A", align 1
