@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1143 = private unnamed_addr constant [1364 x i8] c"## What it does\0ACheck for `typing.Optional` annotations that can be rewritten based on [PEP 604] syntax.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A[PEP 604] introduced a new syntax for union type annotations based on the\0A`|` operator. This syntax is more concise and readable than the previous\0A`typing.Optional` syntax.\0A\0AThis rule is enabled when targeting Python 3.10 or later (see:\0A[`target-version`]). By default, it's _also_ enabled for earlier Python\0Aversions if `from __future__ import annotations` is present, as\0A`__future__` annotations are not evaluated at runtime. If your code relies\0Aon runtime type annotations (either directly or via a library like\0APydantic), you can disable this behavior for Python versions prior to 3.10\0Aby setting [`lint.pyupgrade.keep-runtime-typing`] to `true`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom typing import Optional\0A\0Afoo: Optional[int] = None\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afoo: int | None = None\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as it may lead to runtime errors when\0Aalongside libraries that rely on runtime type annotations, like Pydantic,\0Aon Python versions prior to Python 3.10. It may also lead to runtime errors\0Ain unusual and likely incorrect type annotations where the type does not\0Asupport the `|` operator.\0A\0A## Options\0A- `target-version`\0A- `lint.pyupgrade.keep-runtime-typing`\0A\0A[PEP 604]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0604/\0A", align 1
