@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1184 = private unnamed_addr constant [757 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for consecutive calls to `append`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AConsecutive calls to `append` can be less efficient than batching them into\0Aa single `extend`. Each `append` resizes the list individually, whereas an\0A`extend` can resize the list once for all elements.\0A\0A## Known problems\0AThis rule is prone to false negatives due to type inference limitations,\0Aas it will only detect lists that are instantiated as literals or annotated\0Awith a type annotation.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Anums = [1, 2, 3]\0A\0Anums.append(4)\0Anums.append(5)\0Anums.append(6)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Anums = [1, 2, 3]\0A\0Anums.extend((4, 5, 6))\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: More on Lists](https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#more-on-lists)\0A", align 1
